From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Yevhen Salitrynskyi <ysalitrynskyi@gmail.com>
Date: Fri, 6 Nov 2026 14:22:07 -0500
Subject: feat: sync the sidebar through WebDAV or S3 storage the user sets up

Sync went through a folder, so it needed a sync app or a shared drive to
carry that folder. A profile can now use a WebDAV folder or an S3 bucket the
user already has instead. Nothing for it ships: the user types an address and
their own sign-in, and a test must pass before the join sheet appears. It signs
in, proves the place is what it says, and writes, reads back, lists and deletes
a probe. A place anyone can read with no sign-in is refused.

A bridge beside the service keeps a mirror of the place in the profile and the
folder preference names that mirror, so the merge, the file code, the ledger
and the sheet are the folder's, and only a read or a write goes through the
bridge first. A file a list shows unchanged is not downloaded, and a place has
no watch, so it is read at most every 30 seconds on activation and every 60
while a window is in front, because each request can cost the user money.

Requests go through the browser's own network context with SimpleURLLoader: no
cookies, no referrer, no cache, and nothing a page, an extension or DevTools
sees. S3's signature is Chromium's hash and HMAC, a server's XML goes through
the sandboxed data decoder, a response stops at its cap, and a redirect that
leaves the host or drops encryption is refused. The sign-in is sealed by the
system's key store (os_crypt_async: this pin has no synchronous OSCrypt), and
nothing is kept where that is not available.

Why: BACKLOG S-426, ADR 0032, docs/features/sidebar-file.md Sf43-Sf57.

Removable when: never as a feature.
